==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[mean time]]

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}}, as {{compound|mean|time}}. Adverb is by [[ellipsis]] from {{term||in the meantime}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|mēnʹtīm}}, {{IPA|/ˈmiːntaɪm/}}, {{X-SAMPA|/"mi:ntaIm/}}
* {{audio|en-us-meantime.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# The [[time]] spent [[waiting]] for another event; time in [[between]].
#: ''I'll get started tomorrow, but in the '''meantime''' let's see if we can get a few more opinions.''

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|between-time}}

====Translations====
{{trans-top}}
* Dutch: {{t+|nl|ondertussen}}
* French: [[pendant ce temps]]
* Italian: {{t|it|frattempo|m}}, {{t+|it|attesa}}
{{trans-mid}}
* Latin: {{t|la|interea}}, {{t|la|interim}}
* Serbo-Croatian: {{t|sh|međuvrijeme|n}}
* Spanish: {{t+|es|entretanto|m}}
{{trans-bottom}}

====Derived terms====
* [[for the mean time]], [[for the meantime]]
* [[in mean time]], [[in meantime]]
* [[in the mean time]], [[in the meantime]]
* [[this mean time]], [[this meantime]]

====Related terms====
* [[meanwhile]]

===Adverb===
{{en-adv}}

# [[during]] the [[interval]]; [[meanwhile]]
#* '''1919''', [[w:Boris Sidis|Boris Sidis]], ''[[s:The Source and Aim of Human Progress|The Source and Aim of Human Progress]]'':
#*: '''''Meantime''' Pope Urban II convoked two councils, one after another.''
#* '''1934''', [[w:Rex Stout|Rex Stout]], [[w:Fer-de-Lance (book)|''Fer-de-Lance'']], 1992 [[w:Bantam Books|Bantam]] edition, ISBN 0553278193, page 97:
#*: Lunch will be in twenty minutes. '''Meantime''': I have{{...}}

====Usage notes====
Usually appears sentence-initially.

